Kubernetes

I. Types of Kubernetes Pods

•	Single Container Pod: Contains only one application container.
•	Multi-Container Pod: Houses multiple containers working together.
•	Init Container Pods: Specialized pods that run initialization tasks before the main application containers start.
•	Static Pods: Managed directly by the kubelet on a specific node rather than by the API server.


II. Init Containers 

•	Purpose: Used for initialization, such as waiting for a database to become ready or preparing configuration files.
•	Behavior:
•	Init containers run to completion before any application containers start.
•	If multiple init containers exist, they execute sequentially.
•	Once successfully executed, they terminate and are removed.
•	Configuration: Defined under the `initContainers` field in the Pod manifest.
•	Volume Mounting: Can be used to share data (e.g., configuration files) between init containers and application containers using shared `volumes`.

III. Static Pods 

•	Definition: Pods created and managed directly by the `kubelet` on a specific worker node.
•	Mechanism: The `kubelet` monitors a specific manifest directory on the node; any YAML file placed there is automatically detected and the pod is created.
•	Management: To delete a static pod, the manifest file must be removed from the predefined directory on the node.
•	Best Practice: Not recommended for real-time scenarios because manual access to worker nodes is generally restricted.

 Pod Restart Policies 

•	Always (Default): Kubernetes will always restart the container, regardless of the exit code (success or failure).
•	OnFailure: The container is only restarted if it exits with a non-zero (error) status code.
•	Never: The container is never restarted, even if it fails.
•	CrashLoopBackOff: Occurs when a container fails repeatedly, causing Kubernetes to enter a back-off loop for restarts.

V. Troubleshooting Pod Failures 

•	Common Causes: Configuration mistakes in the YAML file, resource exhaustion on nodes, or incorrect image versions/names.
•	Debugging Commands:
•	`kubectl describe pod `: Inspects events and status to identify the root cause of failure.
•	`kubectl get pod  -o yaml`: Views the actual configuration of a running pod.
•	Image Pull Issues:
•	`ErrImagePull`: The system is attempting to pull the image but is unable to.
•	`ImagePullBackOff`: The system has tried to pull the image multiple times and is currently in a wait state.
•	Image Pull Policies:
•	Always: Pulls the image every time the pod starts.
•	IfNotPresent: Pulls the image only if it is not already available locally.
•	Never: Only uses locally existing images.


Kubernetes services concept

I. Introduction to Kubernetes Services 

•	Purpose: Pods are ephemeral and receive unique, changing IP addresses. Services provide a stable network endpoint to expose applications running in pods.
•	Internal Operation: Kubernetes uses kube-proxy to manage networking and forward requests to pods.
•	Service Identification: Services use selectors to identify pods based on labels (key-value pairs) defined in the pod manifest.

II. Types of Kubernetes Services 

•	ClusterIP: The default service type; exposes the application only within the cluster.
•	NodePort: Exposes the service on a specific port on every worker node's IP, allowing external access to the cluster.
•	LoadBalancer: Used for exposing services to the outside world, typically integrated with cloud provider load balancers.
•	ExternalName: Maps a service to an external DNS name.

III. Lab: ClusterIP and Pod-to-Pod Communication 

•	Goal: Enable communication between an nginx pod and an httpd pod using services.
•	Key Commands:
•	`kubectl get pods`: List running pods.
•	`kubectl get service`: List available services.
•	`kubectl get endpoints`: View the IP addresses associated with a service.
•	`kubectl exec -it  -- /bin/sh`: Access the shell of a running pod.
•	`apt install curl`: Install utility to test connectivity from within the pod.
•	`curl http://`: Test communication between services.

IV. Lab: NodePort Service 

•	Concept: Maps a port in the range 30000-32767 on all worker nodes to the service.
•	Workflow:
    1.  Define `type: NodePort` in the service manifest.
    2.  If no `nodePort` is specified, Kubernetes assigns a random port in the allowed range.
    3.  Update the Security Group of the worker nodes to allow inbound traffic on the selected `nodePort`.
    4.  Access the application using the Worker Node Public IP:NodePort.

V. Syntax-Annotated Code Blocks 

yaml

Example NodePort Service Manifest

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: zomato-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
     app: zomato-app    # Must match pod label
  ports:
    - port: 80         # Internal service port
       targetPort: 80   # Container port
       nodePort: 31000  # External port (30000-32767)


This session provides a practical guide to Kubernetes Services and Namespaces, followed by an introduction to Kubernetes Controllers.

1. Kubernetes Services: LoadBalancer 

Building on previous sessions (ClusterIP and NodePort), the presenter explains the LoadBalancer service type. This automates the creation of cloud-native load balancers (AWS, Azure, or GCP) to expose applications to the internet.

Manifest configuration for LoadBalancer:
yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: zomato-service
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: zomato
  ports:
     - protocol: TCP
        port: 80
        targetPort: 3000

2. Kubernetes Namespaces 

Namespaces provide logical isolation for resources, essential for separating environments (e.g., Dev, Test, Prod) or different project workloads within a single cluster.

•	Key Namespace Commands:
•	`kubectl create ns `: Create a new namespace.
•	`kubectl get pods -n `: List resources in a specific namespace.
•	`kubectl delete ns `: Deletes the namespace and all contained resources.
•	`kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=`: Switch the default namespace.

3. Kubernetes Controllers: Replication Controller (RC) 

Controllers manage pod lifecycles and ensure the desired state matches the observed state through a process called reconciliation.

Replication Controller (RC) Example:
RCs ensure a specific number of pod replicas are running. If a pod is deleted, the RC automatically recreates it.

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ReplicationController
metadata:
  name: zomato-rc
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    app: zomato
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: zomato
spec:
  containers:
  -	name: zomato-container
    image: my-zomato-image

Common Controller Commands:

•	`kubectl get rc`: View replication controllers.
•	`kubectl scale rc  --replicas=`: Dynamically scale the number of pods
